PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E}

The requirements demand equal voting control (via directors and/or shares), Tyler having access to dividend-eligible shares, Alex being free to draw salary (no requirement that his shares themselves carry dividends), and a class of non-voting shares that still carry meaningful economic rights so outside investors can be admitted without gaining a vote.

Only choice E supplies a non-voting class (Class C) that is explicitly dividend-eligible and participates in assets up to a redemption value; that class can therefore be issued to future investors. Both Alex and Tyler receive voting, dividend-eligible shares and start as directors, satisfying the control and payment preferences. The remaining choices either (a) authorize no economically useful non-voting class at all or (b) assign the dividend-bearing shares to the wrong person. The presence of Tyler’s sons as initial directors is a minor deviation from perfect equality, but it is the only structure that meets every other stated requirement simultaneously.}

