PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Option A remains the cleanest fit after weighing the objections. Colleague 1 and 3 correctly note that C creates an extra non-voting class, yet that class is stripped of every economic right—dividends, liquidation proceeds, and voting—so it cannot realistically attract outside investors who expect at least some residual claim. In contrast, A’s Class C, while also non-voting, is authorized within a structure where future amendments can readily attach economic rights without disturbing the founders’ 50/50 board deadlock.  

The holding-company issuance in A preserves exact parity: Alex and his wife control the corporation that holds the Class B block, so board votes and shareholder votes remain deadlocked unless both partners agree, satisfying equal control. Alex can still receive salary as a director or employee of the operating company, independent of any dividend right on the shares; the fact that his Class B block is held indirectly does not change that statutory route. Tyler receives dividends directly on his identical Class A shares.  

B’s Class C is marginally better on economics but still leaves the dividend/non-dividend split ambiguous, while E introduces two extra directors and thereby breaks equal control from day one. D lacks any non-voting class at all. Thus A alone meets every stated requirement without unnecessary complexity or dilution of founder parity.}

